+# Unified `cc1` milestone 1 report — 2026-08-01
+
+## Outcome
+
+Milestone 1 is complete.  The configured i686 C `cc1` implementation closure
+is known exactly, the conventional build has a stable `-j1` baseline, and the
+first include-only GCC/TCC prototype has converted the major unity-build risks
+into reviewable header, symbol, and generated-input inventories.
+
+Recommendation: **proceed with targeted boundary work**, but do not yet make
+broad include-guard changes.  The prototype demonstrates a credible speed
+path (GCC parses the complete include closure in about 10 seconds versus a
+72-second conventional median), while also proving that a literal concatenated
+include list cannot compile without deliberate translation-unit boundaries.
+Ten seconds is diagnostic syntax time, not a successful unified executable
+build, so it is not claimed as a 7.2x delivered speedup.
+
+## Reproducible conventional reference
+
+The isolated configured build is outside the repository at:
+
+```text
+/home/foo/src/unified_cc1_artifacts/reference-build
+```
+
+It is configured for `i686-pc-linux-gnu`, C only, without NLS, multilib, or
+bootstrap.  Its `cc1` is a working 32-bit i386 ELF and passes a compile/link/run
+smoke test when its build-private fixed include directory is supplied.
+
+The checked-in `build.sh` provides the same conventional setup for users
+starting in any out-of-tree directory.  It and all milestone tooling use only
+normal GCC build dependencies: POSIX shell, Make, awk, sed, core utilities,
+the C compiler, and binutils.  No Python, optional Perl code, downloaded tool,
+or `#pragma once` is used.
+
+## Conventional `-j1` benchmark
+
+The benchmark retains configure and generator outputs but removes every
+object/archive needed by normal `cc1`, then rebuilds libiberty, libcpp,
+libdecnumber, and `gcc/cc1` serially.  This avoids charging target libgcc and
+unrelated top-level products while preventing incremental no-op results.
+
+```text
+unreported warm-up: 74 s
+measured runs:       72, 72, 74, 72, 70 s
+median:              72 s
+range:               70–74 s
+cc1 size:            21,832,796 bytes in every run
+```
+
+`/usr/bin/time` is absent in Etch, so peak RSS is unavailable and was not
+invented.  Raw command logs and timing rows are retained under
+`/home/foo/src/unified_cc1_artifacts/`.
+
+## Exact implementation closure
+
+The link map selects 287 implementation files:
+
+```text
+direct C front-end/main/generated objects       29
+backend source/target/generated objects        218
+libcpp objects                                  14
+libdecnumber objects                             6
+libiberty objects                               20
+```
+
+Only 20 of libiberty's 49 archive members and 218 of libbackend's 224 members
+are linked.  `gcc/cc1-unified-sources.manifest` records exact include order
+and origin.  `gcc/cc1-unified-generated.manifest` hashes the 82 configured
+inputs reached by the prototype, including target machine output, gengtype
+headers, options, and component configuration headers.
+
+## Header and symbol findings
+
+The GCC `-H` trace observed 233 project headers: 129 were conservatively
+recognized as guarded, 87 require unguarded/manual review, and 17 are
+deliberate multiple-inclusion candidates such as `.def` files.  The audit
+records header opens, not necessarily repeated body preprocessing for guarded
+files.
+
+The conventional-object `nm` audit found 76 file-scope function/data names
+present in at least two objects.  Examples include the C preprocessor callback
+names shared by `c-lex.c` and `c-ppoutput.c`, scheduler helpers shared by
+`sched-ebb.c` and `sched-rgn.c`, and IPA visitor helpers shared by three IPA
+passes.  This audit cannot see typedef, tag, enum, or macro collisions; the
+compiler diagnostics supply that second half of the inventory.
+
+## Prototype failures and interpretation
+
+The include-only prototype contains the complete 287-file closure in derived
+link order.  Stock GCC reaches the end of that closure under diagnostic
+`-fsyntax-only -H` processing in about 10 seconds but reports many collisions.
+TCC stops at the first repeated definition in `except.h` after
+`c-semantics.c`.
+
+The first failure classes are:
+
+- headers designed for repeated use by separate translation units, including
+  `except.h` and `c-incpath.h`;
+- duplicate file-scope static functions/data, including callbacks in
+  `c-lex.c` and `c-ppoutput.c`;
+- macro state leaking across former translation-unit boundaries—for example,
+  `c-pretty-print.h` redefines `pp_base`, corrupting assumptions in later
+  generic pretty-printer code;
+- component-specific `config.h` contexts and build defines that a single
+  preprocessor invocation must switch explicitly; and
+- names from unrelated implementation files that were legal only because
+  each file previously had its own C scope.
+
+These results support the plan's boundary-header approach: retain ordinary
+non-unified builds, add explicit unity-only state restoration/renaming around
+source includes, and guard only headers whose semantics allow one-time body
+inclusion.  Deliberately repeatable definition headers must keep their normal
+behavior.
+
+## Checked-in milestone artifacts
+
+- `contrib/analyze-cc1-unified.sh`: derive the selected implementation closure.
+- `contrib/generate-cc1-unified-prototype.sh`: emit the diagnostic include list.
+- `contrib/audit-cc1-unified-headers.sh`: classify reachable header behavior.
+- `contrib/audit-cc1-unified-symbols.sh`: inventory file-scope collisions.
+- `contrib/manifest-cc1-unified-generated.sh`: hash configured inputs.
+- `contrib/benchmark-cc1-j1.sh`: reproduce the conventional clean-object test.
+- `gcc/cc1-unified-*.manifest` and `.audit`: exact recorded evidence.
+- `WORKLOG-2026-08-01.md`: chronological issues, resolutions, and takeover log.
+
+## First work after this checkpoint
+
+Milestone 2 should establish a small explicit boundary mechanism and make the
+prototype pass successively through the earliest GCC and TCC failures.  Start
+with `except.h`, component configuration switching, and `pp_base` restoration;
+then regenerate both audits after every narrowly grouped change.  Do not mass
+guard or rename all 76 symbols in advance—the actual compiler path should
+decide which changes are necessary and preserve conventional builds after each
+commit.
