+# Single-translation-unit i686 `cc1` build
+
+`gcc/cc1_unified.c` and `libiberty/libiberty_unified.c` directly include the
+complete 288-file C compiler link closure.  The build script compiles each
+unity driver exactly once.  It does not split
+the amalgamation into chunks, invoke `configure`, or require a prior
+conventional GCC build.
+
+## Build from an empty directory
+
+```sh
+mkdir -p /home/foo/src/builds/cc1-unified
+cd /home/foo/src/builds/cc1-unified
+../../gcc-4.2.1/build-cc1-unified.sh
+```
+
+The result is `unified-artifacts/cc1_unified`.  The script also compiles a
+trivial C file as a smoke test and records the single-TU compilation time in
+`unified-artifacts/cc1-unified-compile-seconds`.
+
+The target is intentionally fixed to i686-pc-linux-gnu on Debian Etch.  The
+small compatibility headers in `gcc/unified-i686-config` are the only
+configure-generated files kept in Git.  They record host ABI, target header,
+and standard integer decisions which would otherwise require Autoconf tests.
+
+All other generated material is produced beneath `unified-artifacts`:
+
+- instruction tables derived from `config/i386/i386.md`;
+- option tables derived from the checked-in `.opt` files;
+- GC descriptors produced by `gengtype`;
+- generator executables, objects, archives, and the checksum stub;
+- `cc1_unified.o`, the linked executable, timing result, and smoke-test files.
+- `libiberty_unified.o` and the one-member `libiberty.a` archive.
+
+None of those artifact files belongs in Git.  The fixed
+`gcc/unified-i686-gengtype.files` is a maintained input list, not generated
+output.  Its AWK formatter uses only tools already required by a standard GCC
+4.2.1 build.  The build has no Python, network, or package-install dependency.
+
+## Compiler selection
+
+`CC` selects the compiler used for the one `cc1_unified.c` invocation and the
+final link.  `CC_FOR_BUILD` selects the compiler used for GCC's temporary
+build-machine generators.  By default both use `CC`.
+
+For TCC, use the Etch system GCC for the generator programs and TCC for the
+actual unified compiler:
+
+```sh
+CC=/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc \
+CC_FOR_BUILD=/usr/bin/gcc \
+../../gcc-4.2.1/build-cc1-unified.sh
+```
+
+This is analogous to a normal cross/bootstrap distinction: generator programs
+are build tools and are not pieces of `cc1`.  The log contains one line of the
+form `Compiling gcc/cc1_unified.c once with ...`; there are no chunk compiler
+invocations.
+
+## Unified libiberty
+
+Libiberty is not merely a cc1 implementation detail.  GCC 4.2.1 links it into
+the compiler driver, cc1 and other front ends, collect2, fixincludes, and most
+of GCC's build-time generator programs.  The wider combined GCC/binutils/GDB
+tree also treats it as a shared source library.  It is an internal convenience
+library rather than a stable public ABI, although this release's configured
+`install` target can install `libiberty.a` and selected headers into the GCC
+prefix.
+
+For that reason the standalone path builds the complete Etch-configured
+libiberty archive, not only the members an ordinary static link happens to
+extract for cc1.  `libiberty/libiberty_unified.c` includes the normal
+`REQUIRED_OFILES` set and Etch's configured `mkstemps.c` replacement.  One
+invocation of the compiler selected by `CC` creates
+`libiberty_unified.o`; `ar` places that one object into
+`unified-artifacts/libiberty.a`.  The exact same archive is linked into all
+generator programs and the final unified cc1.  This preserves the conventional
+artifact shape for consumers while retaining a true one-invocation library
+compile.
+
+Three unity-specific compatibility measures are required:
+
+- `cp-demangle.h` now has an ordinary include guard because both demangler
+  implementations include it in the same translation unit.  The guard is
+  harmless in conventional per-file builds.
+- system declarations of `sys_nerr` and `sys_errlist` are hidden at the start
+  of the unity driver.  `strerror.c` already performs this protection in a
+  conventional translation unit, but that is too late after earlier unified
+  sources have included the system headers.
+- `regex.c` is included last.  It deliberately uses host ctype macros whereas
+  the other sources use libiberty's `safe-ctype.h`; placing it last prevents
+  its private macros from changing later source files.
+
+The fixed `gcc/unified-i686-config/libiberty-config.h` was generated once by
+the stock libiberty configure logic inside Etch.  Like the fixed GCC
+configuration headers, it is checked in as the explicitly permitted Autoconf
+compatibility input; the normal unified build runs no configure probes.
+
+`UNIFIED_ARTIFACTS` may select another artifact directory.  `AWK` may select a
+different compatible AWK implementation.
+
+## Current measurements
+
+Fresh empty-directory smoke builds succeeded with both Etch GCC 4.1.2 and TCC
+0.9.27.  Two measured `-O0 -g` GCC single invocations took 27 and 29 seconds;
+the TCC invocation took 2 seconds.  The previously measured equivalent
+285-file conventional `-j1` compile took 59.61 seconds with GCC.  Compared with
+the latest complete unified 29-second invocation, the observed compiler phase
+is about 2.06 times as fast (51.4 percent less wall time).  Generator
+preparation and linking are excluded from both compiler-phase figures.
+
+## Port status and regression checks
+
+Run `../../gcc-4.2.1/report-cc1-unified-progress.sh` to verify the direct include
+closure against `gcc/cc1-unified-sources.manifest`.  The expected result is
+288 of 288 implementation files and zero remaining.
+
+## Full testsuite with unified cc1
+
+Run the following from an out-of-tree directory:
+
+```sh
+../../gcc-4.2.1/check-cc1-unified.sh
+```
+
+If `unified-artifacts/cc1_unified` does not exist, the wrapper builds it first.
+It then builds the conventional `xgcc`, collect2, libgcc, headers, specs, and
+DejaGnu support needed by the standard GCC test harness.  It temporarily
+replaces only the harness's `gcc/cc1` with the unified executable, prints and
+compares their SHA-256 values, runs the full `make -k check-gcc`, checks
+`gcc.sum` against the recorded known results, and restores the conventional
+cc1 even if the run is interrupted.
+
+The established conventional testsuite expectations remain recorded in
+`gcc/testsuite/cc1-unified-baseline.txt` and
+`gcc/testsuite/cc1-unified-known-nonpasses.txt`.  After a testsuite run, use
+`contrib/compare-cc1-unified-testsuite.sh` to reject new failures, unexpected
+passes, errors, or changes to the known non-passing set.
