+# Plan for an include-based unified GCC 4.2.1 `cc1`
+
+Status: proposed for review.  Apart from initializing this Git repository,
+committing the vanilla source on `master`, and creating the
+`unified_cc1` branch, no implementation described below has begun.
+
+## Objective
+
+Add a `gcc/cc1_unified.c` translation unit which builds the i686 Linux GCC
+4.2.1 C compiler proper by textually including all implementation files
+normally linked into `cc1`.
+
+The target user operation is deliberately literal:
+
+```sh
+cd /home/foo/src/gcc-4.2.1/gcc
+gcc cc1_unified.c -o cc1
+```
+
+The same source must also build with the TCC already installed in the Etch
+chroot:
+
+```sh
+cd /home/foo/src/gcc-4.2.1/gcc
+/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc cc1_unified.c -o cc1
+```
+
+That command must:
+
+- perform one C compiler invocation;
+- compile all required GCC, libcpp, libdecnumber, and libiberty C code as one
+  translation unit;
+- use no prebuilt GCC object files or GCC support archives;
+- require no `-I`, `-D`, or library arguments beyond those shown;
+- produce an i386/i686 Linux `cc1` which reads preprocessed or integrated-
+  preprocessor C input and emits assembly; and
+- continue to work when the command's `gcc` is the resulting unified
+  compiler, with the normal assembler/linker driver used to finish the next
+  executable;
+- build successfully with both Etch's stock GCC 4.1.2 and TCC 0.9.27; and
+- beat a clean conventional `make -j1` `cc1` build by a wide measured
+  margin.
+
+This is an include-based unity build.  It is not the previous fully flattened
+amalgamation design: project source and header files remain separate, and
+`cc1_unified.c` contains `#include` directives for the required `.c` files.
+
+The speed requirement is comparative.  The unified build itself is one
+compiler process, but it must still be substantially faster in elapsed time
+than the conventional many-object build run with one job.  A compile taking
+only a few seconds is the desired outcome, but measurement against a clean
+`make -j1` build is the acceptance evidence.
+
+## Baseline already established
+
+The source tree is:
+
+```text
+/home/foo/src/gcc-4.2.1
+```
+
+It is the unmodified GCC 4.2.1 release tree in the Debian Etch i386 chroot.
+`MD5SUMS` verifies the release contents.  The existing conventional
+out-of-tree build is:
+
+```text
+/home/foo/src/build
+```
+
+Its `gcc/cc1` is a working 32-bit i386 executable configured as
+`i686-pc-linux-gnu` and built by Debian's GCC 4.1.2.
+
+The Git layout is now:
+
+```text
+master       vanilla GCC 4.2.1 import
+unified_cc1  development branch for this work
+```
+
+The common baseline commit is:
+
+```text
+b1e0a740  Import vanilla GCC 4.2.1
+```
+
+All unity-specific source changes and generated target inputs will be made
+only on `unified_cc1`.
+
+## Scope
+
+The unified program is the C compiler proper, `cc1`, not:
+
+- the `gcc` driver;
+- the C++ or Objective-C front ends;
+- the assembler or linker;
+- target runtime libraries such as `libgcc`; or
+- the complete multi-language GCC distribution.
+
+The fixed configuration is:
+
+```text
+build:  i686-pc-linux-gnu
+host:   i686-pc-linux-gnu
+target: i686-pc-linux-gnu
+language: C
+```
+
+Portability to another target or host is not a first-stage goal.  Generated
+target and host files will be clearly labelled as Etch/i686 configuration
+inputs.
+
+## Why generated inputs must be checked in
+
+A vanilla GCC source checkout cannot build `cc1` directly.  Configure and
+GCC's generator programs normally create target-specific inputs including:
+
+- `auto-host.h`, `config.h`, `tm.h`, and `tm_p.h`;
+- `options.c` and `options.h`;
+- `insn-*.c` and `insn-*.h` files derived from the i386 machine description;
+- `genrtl.c` and `genrtl.h`;
+- garbage-collector metadata such as `gtype-desc.c`, `gtype-desc.h`,
+  `gtype-c.h`, `gtyp-gen.h`, and required `gt-*.h` files;
+- `tree-check.h`; and
+- `cc1-checksum.c`.
+
+The existing build contains 139 generated C/header candidates in `build/gcc`.
+Only the subset actually required by C `cc1` will be copied into a dedicated,
+tracked i686 generated-input directory.
+
+Checking in this minimal subset is what will let the final compile command
+omit a configure/generator step and omit additional include paths.  Each
+copied file will have its origin and original SHA-256 recorded in a manifest.
+
+## Conventional `cc1` closure
+
+The configured Makefile describes normal `cc1` as:
+
+```text
+C_OBJS
++ cc1-checksum.o
++ main.o
++ libbackend.a
++ libcpp.a
++ libiberty.a
++ libdecnumber.a
+```
+
+The implementation closure therefore has four main groups.
+
+### GCC C front end
+
+The `C_OBJS` list supplies `c-lang.c`, `stub-objc.c`, and the shared C/ObjC
+sources for parsing, declarations, type checking, options, diagnostics,
+pragmas, preprocessing, PCH, gimplification, OpenMP, pretty printing, and
+tree dumping.
+
+### GCC language-independent backend
+
+`libbackend.a` currently has 224 members.  It supplies tree and RTL passes,
+data-flow analysis, scheduling, register allocation, debug output, garbage
+collection, interprocedural passes, the pass manager, and the i386 backend.
+
+### Integrated preprocessor and decimal arithmetic
+
+The configured static libraries currently contain:
+
+- 14 `libcpp` members; and
+- 6 `libdecnumber` members.
+
+Their required implementation sources will be included directly.
+
+### Libiberty
+
+The configured `libiberty.a` has 49 members, but a static link need not
+extract every member.  Only members actually selected by the normal `cc1`
+link will be included.  This avoids unnecessary code and unnecessary unity
+name conflicts.
+
+## Planned files
+
+The expected branch additions are:
+
+```text
+UNIFIED_CC1_PLAN.md
+gcc/cc1_unified.c
+gcc/unified-i686/                 fixed generated i686 inputs
+gcc/unified-i686/MANIFEST.sha256
+gcc/unified-boundary.h            unity-only boundary helpers, if needed
+contrib/prepare-cc1-unified.sh    regenerate/verify fixed inputs and manifest
+contrib/test-cc1-unified.sh       build, smoke test, and self-host comparison
+README.cc1-unified.md
+```
+
+If separate boundary headers prove unnecessary, they will not be created.
+No generated objects, executables, assembly, compiler dumps, or logs will be
+committed.
+
+## Phase 1: freeze and verify the conventional reference
+
+1. Record:
+   - the vanilla baseline commit;
+   - the existing configure arguments;
+   - the conventional `cc1` version and ELF identity;
+   - the hashes of the conventional `cc1` and its input archives; and
+   - the source-tree `MD5SUMS` result.
+2. Rebuild conventional `cc1` from the unchanged source in the existing
+   build directory.
+3. Compile a small C smoke-test corpus to assembly with the conventional
+   compiler and save results outside the source repository.
+4. Relink `cc1-dummy`/`cc1` with an ELF linker map and archive tracing enabled.
+5. Stop if the conventional rebuild or smoke tests do not pass.  Unity work
+   must not hide an ordinary build problem.
+
+## Phase 2: derive an exact ordered source manifest
+
+1. Read the configured Makefile values for:
+   - `C_OBJS`;
+   - `OBJS-common`;
+   - `OBJS-archive`;
+   - `out_object_file` and host-hook objects;
+   - `BACKEND`;
+   - `LIBS`; and
+   - the final `cc1` rule.
+2. Record archive member order with `ar t`.
+3. Use the reference link map to determine which `libcpp`, `libdecnumber`,
+   and especially `libiberty` members are actually extracted.
+4. Map every selected object to exactly one source `.c` file or generated
+   `.c` file.
+5. Fail on an unmapped object, ambiguous mapping, missing input, or source
+   outside the GCC 4.2.1 tree and configured build tree.
+6. Produce an ordered manifest.  This manifest, rather than a hand-written
+   approximation, will determine the `.c` include list.
+
+Ordering will begin with support libraries and common declarations needed by
+the compiler, followed by the GCC backend and C front end, and will end with
+the normal `main.c` entry point.  The initial order will be adjusted only when
+C declaration requirements or macro state demand it, with every adjustment
+documented.
+
+## Phase 3: copy the minimal fixed generated configuration
+
+1. Copy only reachable generated C/header inputs from `/home/foo/src/build`
+   to `gcc/unified-i686/`.
+2. Remove build timestamps or absolute temporary paths only when they would
+   prevent reproducibility; do not silently change semantic configuration.
+3. Record each source path and before/after hash in the generated-input
+   manifest.
+4. Verify a second preparation run produces no Git diff.
+5. Include generated files from `cc1_unified.c` using repository-relative
+   quoted paths.
+
+`cc1-checksum.c` requires special handling because the normal build computes
+it from `cc1-dummy`.  Initially, freeze the checksum from the verified
+conventional Etch/i686 build.  Test PCH behavior and self-host reproducibility
+before deciding whether the unified compiler should use a distinct fixed
+checksum.
+
+## Phase 4: make the bare command's header lookup self-contained
+
+The exact command cannot rely on the normal Makefile's `-I` flags.  The
+source layout must therefore satisfy every quoted include by itself.
+
+The plan is:
+
+1. Place `cc1_unified.c` in `gcc/`, where ordinary GCC implementation files
+   already find most GCC-local headers.
+2. Make `cc1_unified.c` define the normal compilation markers before any
+   implementation include:
+
+   ```c
+   #define IN_GCC 1
+   #define HAVE_CONFIG_H 1
+   #define GCC_UNIFIED_CC1 1
+   ```
+
+3. Include the fixed GCC host/target configuration from `unified-i686/`
+   before implementations.
+4. Add narrowly scoped forwarding headers or `GCC_UNIFIED_CC1` include-path
+   alternatives where libcpp, libdecnumber, or libiberty normally depend on
+   Makefile `-I` options.
+5. Do not use environment variables such as `CPATH` to make the acceptance
+   command pass.
+6. Do not install headers into `/usr/include` or modify the Etch chroot's
+   compiler installation.
+7. Audit the successful compilation with `gcc -v` and preprocessor include
+   tracing to prove that no undeclared project include path was supplied
+   externally.
+
+The support libraries have independently generated files named `config.h`.
+They cannot simply be copied over one another, and their macros persist in a
+single translation unit.  The implementation will give their fixed configs
+distinct names, include them at explicit component boundaries, and diagnose
+conflicting macro values before accepting a merged configuration.
+
+### Recursive include-once policy
+
+Do not use `#pragma once` anywhere in this work.
+
+The preferred method is the ordinary portable C preprocessor guard:
+
+```c
+#ifndef GCC42_EXAMPLE_H
+#define GCC42_EXAMPLE_H
+
+/* Header contents.  */
+
+#endif /* GCC42_EXAMPLE_H */
+```
+
+This method naturally has the required behavior.  If `foo.c` is included
+first and includes `inc1.h` and `inc2.h`, their guard macros remain defined
+for the rest of the unified translation unit.  When the later included
+`bar.c` asks for the same headers, their contents are skipped.  In a normal
+non-unified build, each `.c` file starts a new preprocessing translation unit,
+so the guard macros start undefined and both `foo.c` and `bar.c` still receive
+the declarations they require.
+
+The implementation policy is therefore:
+
+1. Let the first natural include in the ordered `.c` sequence pull in a
+   header.  Do not pre-include every header in a giant prefix list, because
+   that could change macro-dependent header semantics and declaration order.
+2. Reuse an existing conventional include guard unchanged wherever the
+   header already has one.
+3. Before changing a header without a guard, classify it as either:
+   - semantically include-once; or
+   - deliberately multi-included.
+4. Add a conventional, globally unique `#ifndef` guard only to a header that
+   is proven semantically include-once.
+5. Verify that adding the guard does not change the conventional Makefile
+   build or any existing same-translation-unit repeated include.
+6. Do not add a global guard to intentionally repeated definition/template
+   files such as applicable `.def`, `.inc`, machine-description, option,
+   mode, or macro-expansion inputs.  Those files must be included at each
+   semantically required site with their expected setup macros, followed by
+   explicit macro cleanup.
+7. Include every implementation `.c` from `cc1_unified.c` exactly once.
+   Internal recursive self-inclusion used deliberately by an implementation
+   is a separately audited exception, not something to suppress blindly.
+8. Add a test that fails if `#pragma once` is introduced anywhere by this
+   branch.
+
+Before extensive source edits, produce a header audit containing:
+
+- every recursively reachable project/generated header;
+- its existing guard macro, if any;
+- every include site in the selected `cc1` closure;
+- whether it is include-once or deliberately multi-included;
+- its observed preprocessing count in the unity prototype; and
+- any proposed new guard or boundary cleanup.
+
+Use GCC preprocessor include tracing and explicit source-boundary markers to
+confirm that guarded header bodies are processed once.  TCC must then compile
+the same guarded structure; no compiler-specific `#pragma` or include-cache
+behavior may be required.
+
+This guard audit and the initial collision report form a review checkpoint.
+The proposed method, number of header changes, deliberately repeated files,
+and expected collision work will be reported before broad mechanical changes
+are made.
+
+## Phase 5: create the first `cc1_unified.c`
+
+The wrapper will contain only:
+
+- a descriptive header and fixed build markers;
+- fixed configuration/generated-header includes;
+- boundary macros or boundary-header includes; and
+- one ordered `#include` for every required implementation `.c` file.
+
+It will not paste or generate the bodies of the included `.c` files.
+
+The first prototype will be compiled with the exact target command:
+
+```sh
+cd gcc
+gcc cc1_unified.c -o cc1
+```
+
+Compiler errors will be captured as the unity conflict worklist.
+
+## Phase 6: find and repair translation-unit collisions
+
+Normal GCC depends heavily on separate compilation.  Combining its source
+files will expose several classes of conflict:
+
+- duplicate `static` functions and variables;
+- tentative definitions with the same name;
+- duplicate typedefs, enum constants, and structure tags;
+- libiberty helpers sharing names with GCC or libcpp helpers;
+- implementation-local macros leaking into later included files;
+- conflicting component configuration macros; and
+- i386 target macros, especially `REG_OK_STRICT` and address-validation
+  definitions, retaining the wrong state across former `.c` boundaries.
+
+The repair policy is:
+
+1. Generate an initial duplicate-local-symbol report with `nm` from the
+   conventional objects.
+2. Let the C compiler identify type, tag, and macro conflicts that ELF symbol
+   inspection cannot see.
+3. Prefer deterministic, narrow fixes:
+   - rename a genuinely file-local identifier;
+   - `#undef` an implementation-local macro at a documented boundary;
+   - explicitly restore strict or non-strict target macro definitions;
+   - suppress a duplicate implementation not selected by the normal link;
+   - make a declaration unambiguously `extern`; or
+   - guard a unity-only adjustment with `GCC_UNIFIED_CC1`.
+4. Keep normal separate compilation behavior unchanged when
+   `GCC_UNIFIED_CC1` is not defined.
+5. Do not globally redefine `static`, weaken types, suppress all diagnostics,
+   or use linker tricks to conceal multiple definitions.
+6. Give every source modification its own explanation in
+   `README.cc1-unified.md`.
+7. After each group of fixes, rebuild both:
+   - the unified translation unit with the bare command; and
+   - the conventional `cc1` with its Makefile.
+8. Compile the unified translation unit with both:
+   - `/usr/bin/gcc` from Debian Etch; and
+   - `/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc`.
+9. Keep TCC compatibility changes under `GCC_UNIFIED_CC1` where they are
+   specific to unity mode.  If a correction is valid for ordinary GCC 4.2.1
+   source as well, verify it in the normal separate build before leaving it
+   unconditional.
+10. Treat a compiler-specific workaround as a documented compatibility fix,
+    not as permission to maintain two different `.c` include manifests.
+
+## Phase 7: prove the executable is a real `cc1`
+
+After `gcc cc1_unified.c -o cc1` succeeds:
+
+1. Repeat the build with both stock Etch GCC and TCC 0.9.27.
+2. Verify with `file` and `readelf` that both outputs are 32-bit i386 ELF
+   executables.
+3. Verify both `./cc1 --version` outputs report GCC 4.2.1 and
+   i686-pc-linux-gnu.
+4. Compile the reference smoke-test corpus to assembly with both outputs.
+5. Assemble and link those outputs with the normal Etch driver/binutils.
+6. Execute the resulting test programs.
+7. Compare each unified compiler with conventional `cc1` for:
+   - exit status;
+   - substantive diagnostics;
+   - generated assembly where deterministic; and
+   - executable behavior.
+8. Compare the first self-generated assembly from the GCC-seeded and
+   TCC-seeded unified compilers.  Once both seeds have produced a correct
+   unified `cc1`, their next-stage output for identical input and options
+   should converge.
+9. Exercise at least preprocessing, declarations, integer/floating point,
+   structs/unions/bitfields, control flow, calls, GNU C extensions, inline
+   assembly, and deliberately invalid input.
+
+## Phase 8: test self-hosting and reproducibility
+
+Run the bootstrap sequence once from the stock-GCC seed and once from the TCC
+seed.  Both sequences will use optimization disabled:
+
+1. The selected seed compiler builds unified `cc1` with the bare command,
+   plus `-O0` only for the controlled comparison run.
+2. Seed unified `cc1` compiles `cc1_unified.c` to stage-2 assembly at `-O0`.
+3. The normal fixed assembler/linker environment creates stage-2 `cc1`.
+4. Stage-2 unified `cc1` compiles the same source, from the same working
+   directory, with identical options and locale, to stage-3 assembly.
+5. `cmp` must report stage-2 and stage-3 assembly byte-for-byte identical.
+6. Hash both assembly files and both executables.
+7. Do not normalize or filter assembly merely to obtain equality.  Any
+   difference is a defect to diagnose.
+8. Require the stock-GCC-seeded and TCC-seeded sequences to reach identical
+   stable assembly, or diagnose and document the seed-dependent difference
+   as a failure.
+
+The acceptance build remains exactly `gcc cc1_unified.c -o cc1`; the
+self-host test script may use `-O0`, explicit output names, and direct `cc1`
+options needed to retain and compare assembly.
+
+## Phase 9: benchmark the unified build against clean `make -j1`
+
+Compilation speed is a primary deliverable, not an incidental benefit.
+Benchmark before accepting extensive cleanup and again after the final
+correct build.
+
+### Compared builds
+
+Measure these cases on the same Etch chroot and host:
+
+1. Conventional clean C `cc1` build with `make -j1`.
+2. Unified `gcc cc1_unified.c -o cc1` using stock Etch GCC.
+3. Unified `/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc cc1_unified.c -o cc1`.
+
+The conventional cases may start from an already configured tree with
+generator outputs present, but all objects and archives contributing to
+`cc1` must be absent.  Generator time will be reported separately and will
+not be selectively charged to only one side.  The unified cases start with
+no unified object or executable and compile the complete translation unit.
+
+### Measurement procedure
+
+1. Record CPU, available memory, storage/filesystem, kernel, chroot identity,
+   and exact compiler versions.
+2. Use wall-clock elapsed time as the primary metric; also record user time,
+   system time, peak resident memory, and output size with the available
+   Etch timing tool.
+3. Run at least five measured repetitions after one unreported warm-up for
+   each warm-cache case and report the median plus range.
+4. Run separately labelled cold-cache tests only if cache state can be reset
+   safely and consistently without privileged global side effects.  Do not
+   claim a cold-cache result from uncontrolled cache conditions.
+5. Keep compiler flags, debug-information policy, optimization level, locale,
+   and linker behavior equivalent.  Record unavoidable differences.
+6. Ensure no unrelated build is competing for CPU or I/O during a measured
+   run.
+7. Preserve raw timing logs outside the tracked source files and summarize
+   results in `README.cc1-unified.md`.
+
+### Performance gate
+
+After the include-guard audit and first minimally compiling unity prototype,
+report:
+
+- initial unified GCC and TCC times;
+- conventional clean `make -j1` time;
+- the speedup factor against the conventional build;
+- time spent preprocessing, compiling, assembling, and linking where the
+  tools can expose it; and
+- the remaining collision/change count.
+
+Pause for review before broad source modifications if the prototype does not
+show a credible path to a wide speed advantage.  The final result must beat
+the conventional `make -j1` build by a wide margin.  The plan does not invent
+an arbitrary percentage before measuring this machine, but it will report a
+ratio rather than describing a marginal difference as success.  A final
+compile time of only a few seconds remains the intended result, especially
+for TCC.
+
+Repeated header parsing is part of the benchmark diagnosis.  Include tracing
+must show that ordinary guarded project headers are processed once in the
+unified translation unit; deliberately multi-included definition/template
+files will be itemized so their cost is visible rather than hidden.
+
+## Phase 10: documentation and testsuite work
+
+Document:
+
+- the exact reference configure/build identity;
+- the complete included implementation manifest;
+- every checked-in generated input and its origin;
+- every unity-only source change and why separate compilation previously
+  made the original valid;
+- remaining system-header and libc dependencies;
+- the literal one-step build command;
+- the self-host procedure and hashes;
+- expected warnings; and
+- the fixed Etch/i686 limitation.
+
+Run a targeted GCC C testsuite subset through the unified `cc1` after smoke
+tests and self-hosting pass.  A full testsuite run is desirable evidence but
+is not required before the first working prototype.  Any regression relative
+to the conventional Etch `cc1` must be recorded and resolved before declaring
+the project complete.
+
+## Tool and dependency policy
+
+Use only software already present in the Etch chroot.  Do not fetch from the
+network and do not install packages.
+
+Before implementation, inventory at least:
+
+- GCC, assembler, linker, `ar`, `nm`, `objdump`, and `readelf`;
+- `/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc`;
+- POSIX shell, `make`, `awk`, `sed`, `cmp`, and checksum tools;
+- Bison/flex only if regeneration proves necessary; and
+- DejaGNU/Tcl/Expect only for the later testsuite phase.
+
+If a required dependency is absent, stop and report it rather than obtaining
+it externally.  Preparation and test scripts will not use here-documents.
+
+The unified build and every checked-in preparation, manifest, guard-audit,
+generation, benchmark, and test script must not add a build dependency beyond
+the tools needed for a standard GCC 4.2.1 build.  In particular, do not
+require Python.  Do not make Perl or another optional scripting runtime a
+unified-build dependency merely because it happens to be installed in this
+chroot.  Prefer POSIX shell, Make, awk, sed, standard file utilities, the C
+compiler, and binutils already used by the normal build.
+
+The initial inspection already confirms these two required C seeds:
+
+```text
+/usr/bin/gcc                 GCC 4.1.2 20061115 (Debian 4.1.1-21)
+/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc      TCC 0.9.27, i386 Linux
+```
+
+## Success criteria
+
+This work is complete only when:
+
+- the normal Makefile build still produces a working conventional `cc1`;
+- `gcc/cc1_unified.c` includes the exact normal C `cc1` implementation
+  closure;
+- the checked-in generated inputs are minimal, reproducible, and documented;
+- from `gcc/`, the literal command `gcc cc1_unified.c -o cc1` succeeds;
+- from `gcc/`, the equivalent literal TCC command succeeds;
+- that command uses no hidden include-path environment or GCC support
+  archives;
+- the output is a functioning i686 Linux GCC 4.2.1 `cc1`;
+- each ordinary recursively included header body is processed only once,
+  except for explicitly documented files whose semantics require repeated
+  inclusion;
+- the branch introduces no `#pragma once` directives;
+- the unified compiler passes the smoke-test comparison;
+- the unified compiler builds itself twice at `-O0`;
+- stage-2 and stage-3 assembly is byte-for-byte identical;
+- stock-GCC-seeded and TCC-seeded bootstraps converge on the same stable
+  assembly;
+- measured unified builds beat a clean conventional `make -j1` `cc1` build
+  by a wide margin;
+- every source change is unity-guarded where possible and documented;
+- the vanilla baseline remains available unchanged on `master`; and
+- no software or data has been downloaded or installed.
+
+## Review points
+
+Please review these proposed decisions before implementation:
+
+1. Put the tracked wrapper at `gcc/cc1_unified.c` and make the exact bare
+   command run from the `gcc/` directory.
+2. Check in the minimal fixed Etch/i686 generated inputs needed to eliminate
+   configure, generator, and `-I` requirements from that command.
+3. Include only archive members selected by the conventional `cc1` link,
+   especially for libiberty.
+4. Guard source-level unity repairs with `GCC_UNIFIED_CC1` wherever possible,
+   preserving the conventional build.
+5. Use conventional include guards, never `#pragma once`, and audit
+   deliberately multi-included files before adding any new guard.
+6. Require both stock Etch GCC and `/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc` to build the same
+   `cc1_unified.c` include manifest.
+7. Establish and review the header/collision audit and initial performance
+   ratios before extensive changes.
+8. Require the unified build to beat a clean conventional `make -j1` build
+   by a wide measured margin.
+9. Require both conventional-build regression checks and byte-identical
+   stage-2/stage-3 self-host assembly before completion.
