+# Unified libiberty and full-check work log — 2026-08-01
+
+## 22:54 BST — request and audit
+
+The standalone script linked a minimal support archive, which was sufficient
+for cc1 but did not represent the complete libiberty product.  The source and
+Makefiles show that libiberty is also used by GCC's driver, collect2,
+fixincludes, build generators, and other programs.  Its Makefile can install
+the archive and selected headers when configured with an installation
+destination.  The unified path therefore needs a reusable full archive.
+
+## 23:01 BST — first full unity attempt (7 minutes)
+
+Added `libiberty/libiberty_unified.c` with the ordinary `REQUIRED_OFILES` set
+plus Etch's configured `mkstemps.c`.  The first compile exposed three
+translation-unit assumptions:
+
+- unguarded `cp-demangle.h` was read by both demangler implementations;
+- `regex.c` included host ctype state before `safe-ctype.h`;
+- `strerror.c` could no longer hide system `sys_errlist` declarations because
+  another source had already included the guarded system header.
+
+The first diagnostic command was accidentally run using the host compiler and
+Jammy headers against Etch configuration.  That result was discarded; all
+subsequent validation was explicitly run in the Etch bubblewrap root.
+
+## 23:10 BST — GCC validation (9 minutes)
+
+Added a conventional include guard to `cp-demangle.h`, moved `regex.c` to the
+end of the unity driver, and hid the two system error-table declarations at
+the unity translation-unit boundary.  Keeping safe-ctype's macros alive until
+the last source also resolved undefined `ISBLANK`/`ISSPACE` references from
+`argv.c`.
+
+Etch GCC 4.1.2 then compiled the complete library in one invocation, archived
+the one resulting object, used that archive for every generator, compiled the
+288-file cc1 closure in one invocation (28 seconds), linked it against the
+same archive, and passed the C smoke compilation.
+
+## 23:12 BST — TCC validation (2 minutes)
+
+Repeated the complete build with TCC 0.9.27 selected for both unity objects
+and the final link, while retaining system GCC for build-machine generators.
+TCC compiled the cc1 unity translation unit in one second.  The resulting
+i386 cc1 linked and passed the same smoke compilation.  No additional
+software, network access, Python, or nonstandard build dependency was used.
+
+The progress reporter was extended to inspect both unity drivers.  It reports
+288 of 288 members of cc1's conventional static-link closure unified.  Extra
+members in the complete libiberty product are deliberately ignored by that
+cc1-closure metric.
+
+## Full-test follow-up
+
+`check-cc1-unified.sh` was added to create the ordinary driver/runtime test
+harness, temporarily substitute the unified cc1, run all C tests, compare the
+result with the checked-in expectation set, and restore the conventional cc1.
+The result of the full run is recorded below after completion.
+
+## 23:24 BST — test-wrapper dependency correction
+
+The first full-check launch built the unified compiler and conventional test
+harness, then verified that the substituted cc1 had the same SHA-256 as the
+unified artifact.  It stopped before DejaGnu because the wrapper tried to run
+the optional `file` utility, which is not installed in the vanilla Etch
+chroot.  The signal/exit trap restored conventional cc1 correctly.  Removed
+that informational command: it was not a GCC build dependency and must not
+become a new unified-build requirement.
+
+## 23:28 BST — first full-suite regression and root cause
+
+The corrected full suite reached DejaGnu with a checksum-verified unified
+cc1, but early optimized torture cases segfaulted.  A host GDB backtrace with
+the Etch root as sysroot showed `rtlanal.c:nonzero_bits1` calling
+`combine.c:reg_nonzero_bits_for_combine` during CSE, after combine had ended.
+
+This was unity macro leakage, not a source-program failure.  `combine.c`
+temporarily redefines `RTL_HOOKS_*` macros to initialize its private hook
+table.  A conventional translation-unit boundary discards those definitions.
+In the unity driver they survived through `cse.c` and caused
+`rtlhooks.c:general_rtl_hooks` to be initialized with combine's callbacks.
+Those callbacks then dereferenced combine state after it had been freed.
+
+The repeatable core boundary now restores every `RTL_HOOKS_*` initializer
+field to the defaults from `rtlhooks-def.h`, and also removes rtlanal.c's
+file-local recursion-poison macro.  The bad full run was stopped so the fix
+could be checked with its minimal reproducer before restarting the suite.
+
+Rebuilt the unified compiler and reran `20000120-2.c` directly at every
+optimization used by `gcc.c-torture`: `-O0`, `-O1`, `-O2`, the three `-O3`
+variants, `-O3 -g`, and `-Os`.  All eight invocations completed after the
+boundary fix; the former `-O1` crash was no longer reproducible.
+
+## 23:32 BST — reload strict-address regression
+
+The restarted suite progressed past the first reproducer, then found an `-O0`
+ICE in `20000804-1.c`: reload left a pseudo-register's memory equivalence
+inside another memory address.  Comparing `-fdump-rtl-all` output from the
+conventional and unified compilers showed identical local-register-allocation
+input.  In the global-reload dump, conventional cc1 loaded the address into a
+hard register while unified cc1 retained the invalid nested `MEM`.
+
+`reload.c` defines `REG_OK_STRICT` before it includes `tm.h`; i386's target
+header consequently gives that conventional translation unit strict address
+predicates.  The unity driver had included guarded `tm.h` much earlier in its
+normal non-strict mode, so reload's later define could not select the alternate
+macros.  Around the reload.c inclusion, the fixed-i686 unity driver now
+selects the strict i386 base/index and legitimate-address predicates
+explicitly, then restores their non-strict forms.  No conventional source or
+build behavior is changed.
+
+After rebuilding, both `20000120-2.c` and `20000804-1.c` completed at all
+eight torture optimization variants.  This checked that the strict reload
+selection fixed the new case without reopening the RTL-hook case.
+
+## 23:38 BST — C front-end macro collides with fold local
+
+The next full restart reached `20010408-1.c` before optimized variants
+segfaulted in `fold_convert`.  GDB showed that two identical integer offsets
+entered `fold_comparison`, but converting the first changed the adjacent
+second offset into an `INTEGER_TYPE` pointer.
+
+`c-common.h` defines `signed_size_type_node` as an index macro into
+`c_global_trees`.  `fold-const.c`, which does not include that C-only header in
+a conventional build, uses the same spelling for a block-local scalar.  In
+the unified preprocessor stream its declaration became a local array named
+`c_global_trees`; assigning the signed type through the expanded macro wrote
+past that array and replaced the neighboring offset local.
+
+The unity driver now remaps that spelling to a unique fold-local name only
+while including `fold-const.c`, then restores the C front-end macro.  This is
+a preprocessing-boundary repair; neither implementation source is changed.
+
+After rebuilding, all eight optimization variants passed for all three
+accumulated reproducers: `20000120-2.c`, `20000804-1.c`, and `20010408-1.c`.
