+# Low-hanging unity migration work log — 2026-08-01
+
+## Selection method
+
+The exact 287-file link closure and the milestone 1 static-symbol audit were
+used as the starting point.  Files participating in a known file-scope symbol
+collision were excluded from the optimistic backend/direct batches.  Remaining
+files were grouped in manifest order, initially eight at a time, and compiled
+as real objects with both Etch GCC 4.1.2 and TCC 0.9.27.
+
+Failing batches were recursively split.  Individually viable files which
+conflicted with an adjacent file were cross-paired with distant candidates.
+Only groups of at least two implementations which compiled under both
+compilers were accepted.  Three named implementations which failed even the
+individual probe (`toplev.c`, `version.c`, and target `i386.c`) were deferred.
+
+An early scratch probe used global shell variables recursively and skipped
+some right-hand subdivisions.  No tracked selection was made from that output.
+The probe was fixed by isolating recursive function state in subshells and the
+entire backend classification was rerun from scratch.
+
+## Accepted implementation files
+
+The checked `gcc/cc1-unified-chunks.manifest` records 203 newly migrated files:
+
+```text
+backend       146
+direct         23
+libcpp         14
+libiberty      20
+```
+
+These join the previous three core-tree files and all six libdecnumber files,
+for 212 of 287 linked implementations in unified mode.  `cc1-checksum.c` was
+explicitly excluded: it is regenerated from `cc1-dummy` at each link and must
+remain its own conventional object.  `main.c` was moved from that rejected pair
+into another successfully revalidated direct chunk.
+
+All selected libcpp and selected libiberty implementations were accepted once
+compiled under their own component configurations.  The remaining
+`decNumber.c`, `decContext.c`, and `decUtility.c` form a separate core-decimal
+chunk so `decNumber.c` retains its minimal extensible representation rather
+than inheriting the fixed-width interchange declaration.
+
+## Build integration
+
+`contrib/generate-cc1-unified-chunks.sh` generates the manifest-defined unity
+drivers in the artifact directory using only POSIX shell and awk.
+`build-cc1-unified.sh` now compiles all chunks, copies and rewrites the four
+participating archives, replaces migrated direct objects through `C_OBJS`, and
+links the five still-conventional direct objects with the unified remainder.
+The normal build-tree `cc1` is protected by an interruption-safe restore trap.
+
+The first integrated GCC link failed because the remaining conventional
+`C_OBJS` names retained newline separators inside one Make argument.  Converting
+that generated list to spaces fixed the Make dependency without changing the
+selection.
+
+Both GCC and TCC builds now produce native i386 `cc1_unified` executables.  Each
+compiled, assembled, linked, and ran the standard smoke input, returned 101,
+and emitted byte-identical assembly with SHA-256:
+
+```text
+c2be4b9929ebf7ed0ec4a4e437ea5181c9a251df09c5b73d6305aac24f45f548
+```
+
+## Validation and timing
+
+The GCC-built 212-file hybrid completed the full C testsuite.  Its exact result
+set matches `gcc/testsuite/cc1-unified-known-nonpasses.txt`:
+
+```text
+PASS          42139
+XFAIL           116
+FAIL              0
+XPASS             0
+UNRESOLVED         1
+UNTESTED          28
+UNSUPPORTED      293
+ERROR              0
+```
+
+During the run, `gcc.c-torture/compile/20001226-1.c -O3` initially appeared to
+be a unity-induced optimizer hang.  Manifest-subset support was added to the
+builder and generator to bisect that possibility.  Direct measurement then
+showed that the untouched conventional compiler has the same very long compile
+time on that input.  The full baseline comparison subsequently confirmed that
+there was no changed test result.
+
+`contrib/benchmark-cc1-unified-current.sh` compiles exactly the same 212
+migrated files with the same `-O0 -g` component flags, first as separate
+translation units and then through the unity drivers.  Three warm-cache GCC
+wall-clock measurements were:
+
+```text
+mode            runs (seconds)        median
+conventional    43.33 39.82 40.42     40.42
+unified         27.34 26.23 26.31     26.31
+```
+
+The current unified portion therefore saves 14.11 seconds, a 34.91 percent
+reduction in wall time, and compiles 1.54 times as fast.  This matched-set
+number deliberately excludes archive rewriting, linking, and the 75 files
+which have not yet moved to unity mode.  The milestone 1 clean conventional
+build of the complete 287-file `cc1` closure remains 72 seconds median; it is
+useful context but is not used to inflate the matched-set speedup.
+
+## Current status
+
+```text
+unified implementation files   212
+total linked implementations   287
+remaining                       75
+progress                        73.87 percent
+```
+
+The complete selection compiles and links with both GCC 4.1.2 and TCC 0.9.27.
+The full regression result above was taken with the GCC-produced hybrid; the
+TCC-produced executable also passed the build/link/run smoke check and emitted
+assembly byte-identical to the GCC-produced executable for that input.
