+# Milestone 2 unity pilot work log — 2026-08-01
+
+## Scope
+
+This is a proof of the planned unity-boundary technique before attempting the
+complete 287-file `cc1` closure.  It deliberately covers two different groups:
+
+- the three libdecnumber interchange implementations (`decimal128.c`,
+  `decimal64.c`, and `decimal32.c`); and
+- three central GCC tree-IR implementations (`tree.c`, `tree-iterator.c`, and
+  `tree-gimple.c`).
+
+The ordinary Makefiles and ordinary object lists remain unchanged.  GCC and
+TCC compile the pilots only through the new unity drivers.
+
+## Decimal pilot
+
+The first literal include attempt failed under both compilers because
+`decNumberLocal.h` deliberately emits an error when included twice.  It had an
+existing `DECNUMBERLOC` marker but used the marker to diagnose repetition
+rather than skip the body.
+
+The fix is deliberately conditional: when `GCC_DECNUMBER_UNIFIED` is defined,
+the existing marker acts as a conventional include guard; without that unity
+marker, a repeated inclusion produces exactly the original error.  This keeps
+the normal build semantics intact.
+
+The interchange implementations must be included widest first.  The public
+headers document this ordering, and it gives the shared `decNumber` declaration
+enough coefficient storage for all three formats.  A repeatable boundary header
+undefines `DECNUMDIGITS` and `DEC_clear`, the two per-file macros which ordinary
+translation-unit termination would discard.
+
+Both Etch GCC 4.1.2 and TCC 0.9.27 compile the resulting unity file.  For each
+compiler, the unified object exports the same 15 global symbols as the union of
+the three separate objects.  Replacing the three members of a copied
+`libdecnumber.a` links a complete 32-bit `cc1` for both objects.
+
+A small format-specific runtime check converts values through decimal32,
+decimal64, and decimal128.  Conventional, GCC-unified, and TCC-unified archives
+produce identical text:
+
+```text
+12345678901234.56789012345678901234
+-123456.7890123456
+7654.321
+```
+
+## Core tree-IR pilot
+
+`tree.c`, `tree-iterator.c`, and `tree-gimple.c` each compiled conventionally
+with GCC and TCC before the unity attempt.  Their include-only unity driver then
+compiled successfully with both compilers without needing a cleanup rule.  An
+intentionally repeatable, currently empty boundary header keeps the same
+structure ready for future narrowly justified restorations.
+
+For each compiler, the unity object exports exactly the same 239 global symbols
+as the three separate objects.  Replacing those members in a copied
+`libbackend.a` links a complete native `cc1` with both the GCC-produced and
+TCC-produced unity object.  Conventional and both pilot compilers produced
+byte-identical assembly for the initial compile/link/run smoke input.
+
+## Initial timing evidence
+
+All cases used `-O0 -g`, warm filesystem cache, the same compiler and include
+flags within each comparison, and overwrote artifact objects on every
+iteration.  Etch has no high-resolution external `time`, so repeated builds
+were measured as a batch with `date +%s`.
+
+```text
+group       compiler  iterations  separate  unified  observed ratio
+decimal     GCC       100         11 s      5 s      2.20x
+decimal     TCC       100          3 s      1 s      3.00x
+core tree   GCC        50         36 s     26 s      1.38x
+core tree   TCC        50          7 s      3 s      2.33x
+```
+
+The decimal TCC figures have one-second quantization and should not be treated
+as precise microbenchmarks.  The direction is nevertheless consistent across
+both groups and compilers.  The larger core-GCC sample shows a smaller but still
+material gain because actual compilation work dominates more of the repeated
+header/parser startup cost.
+
+## Reproduction tooling
+
+`contrib/test-cc1-unified-pilots.sh` builds separate and unified objects with
+both compilers, checks exported symbol sets, creates replacement archives,
+runs the focused decimal semantic check, links all four pilot `cc1` variants,
+and restores the conventional build executable.
+
+`contrib/benchmark-cc1-unified-pilots.sh` repeats comparable separate and unity
+compilation and writes a tab-separated timing file to the caller's artifact
+directory.  Both scripts use standard shell/build tools and require no Python,
+Perl, network access, or additional package.
+
+## Validation status
+
+The full GCC C testsuite completed with both TCC-produced unity groups linked
+into the same `cc1`.  Its result is identical to the conventional baseline:
+
+```text
+expected passes       42139
+expected failures       116
+unresolved                1
+untested                  28
+unsupported              293
+FAIL/XPASS/ERROR            0
+```
+
+The sole unresolved result is the already-recorded execution bookkeeping line
+after the expected `mayalias-2.c -O3 -g` ICE.  The conventional `cc1` executable
+was restored after the run.
+
+The working core pilot was promoted to the requested `gcc/cc1_unified.c` name.
+`build-cc1-unified.sh` builds the current partial unified compiler from an
+out-of-tree conventional build, and `report-cc1-unified-progress.sh` validates
+and reports six of 287 linked implementation files (2.09 percent) in unity
+mode.  Exact known testsuite non-passes are tracked under `gcc/testsuite` and
+checked by `contrib/compare-cc1-unified-testsuite.sh`.
