+# Unified cc1 remainder work log — 2026-08-01
+
+## Scope and working rule
+
+This phase attempts every implementation still outside the partial unified
+build after the 212/287 low-hanging checkpoint.  A file is either integrated
+in a GCC- and TCC-compilable unity group or recorded below immediately when a
+specific blocker is confirmed.  A failed candidate is removed from the active
+manifest before the next candidate is tried, so both the conventional build
+and the last accepted unified build remain usable.
+
+Starting state:
+
+```text
+already unified                 212
+remaining to attempt             75
+total linked implementations    287
+```
+
+## Hard-case ledger
+
+### `build:gcc/cc1-checksum.c` (`cc1-checksum.o`)
+
+Status: deferred before batch probing; its build role is already conclusive.
+
+This file is generated during the two-stage `cc1` link.  GCC first links
+`cc1-dummy`, computes a checksum from that executable, emits
+`cc1-checksum.c`, compiles it separately, and links the final `cc1`.  Folding
+the generated file into an earlier unity object would either use a stale
+checksum or introduce a circular dependency on the executable which that
+object is helping to create.  It must remain a conventional generated object
+until the link/checksum protocol itself is redesigned.
+
+### `source:gcc/reload.c` (`reload.o`)
+
+Status: deferred after build success but full-testsuite semantic failure.
+
+`reload.c` defines `REG_OK_STRICT` before including `tm.h`, `rtl.h`, and the
+target predicates.  In a conventional translation unit that definition
+selects the strict register-validity forms while those headers are parsed.  An
+append-only unity placement necessarily follows another implementation which
+has already activated the header guards without `REG_OK_STRICT`; `reload.c`
+therefore compiles and links but silently receives the non-strict inline/macro
+definitions.
+
+The full 286-file regression run exposed 1,052 unexpected failures and 454
+unresolved executions, beginning with an ICE in
+`gcc.c-torture/compile/20000804-1.c -O0` at `recog.c:1994`.  Manifest-prefix
+bisection proved that the first 15 established-chunk additions pass that
+reproducer and adding `reload.c` as the sixteenth fails it.  This is not safe
+to repair with an end-of-file `#undef`: the semantic choice happens while the
+guarded headers are first parsed.  The next porting phase should create a
+reload-first chunk and move a compatible established implementation behind it,
+or split the strict target predicates into an explicitly repeatable header
+section.  For now `reload.o` is restored to conventional compilation.
+
+## Attempt log
+
+The other 74 files are being compiled in real include groups with both Etch
+GCC 4.1.2 and `/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc`.  Failed groups are recursively split;
+cross-pairing and straightforward translation-unit boundary cleanup follow.
+
+### First grouping checkpoint
+
+The recursive probe accepted 34 files immediately.  Thirty-six more compiled
+individually but conflicted with their initial neighbours.  A component-local
+cross-pair search accepted another 16 files, including the direct-front-end
+pair `c-ppoutput.c` plus `tree-mudflap.c`.  The one initially mixed
+direct/backend group was split and revalidated so archive ownership remains
+unambiguous.
+
+Forty-nine new files were added as chunks 060 through 082.  The resulting
+261/287 hybrid builds and links successfully with both GCC and TCC.  This is a
+working checkpoint; the manifest is not carrying any failed experimental
+candidate.
+
+Four individual probes need special handling rather than immediate deferral:
+
+* `prefix.c` needs the normal Makefile `PREFIX` and `BASEVER` definitions.
+* `toplev.c` needs the normal Makefile `TARGET_NAME` definition.
+* `version.c` needs the normal Makefile version/date/development definitions.
+* TCC rejects the tentative incomplete-array declaration of
+  `ix86_attribute_table` in `i386.c`; the later complete definition shows that
+  adding `extern` is the source-compatible correction to test.
+
+Twenty-one individually valid backend files could not be paired with one
+another.  They are not yet hard cases: the next pass attempts to merge each
+with a compatible established backend chunk.
+
+### Established-chunk placement checkpoint
+
+`contrib/place-cc1-unified-remainders.sh` was added to make the second pass
+reproducible.  For each candidate it builds each proposed enlarged established
+chunk with both GCC and TCC, records the first compatible placement, and leaves
+the checked manifest untouched.  It has no dependency beyond the standard GCC
+build tools plus the two compilers under test.
+
+All 21 collision-heavy files found compatible established chunks.  Important
+placements include `expr.c` in backend chunk 006, `function.c` in chunk 010,
+`gcse.c` in chunk 011, and `reload.c`/`reload1.c` in chunks 026/030.  Because
+these additions are intentionally recorded after the original contiguous
+chunk rows, the generator and consumers now use a unique sorted chunk-name
+set rather than relying on adjacent manifest rows.
+
+The resulting 282/287 hybrid builds and links with both GCC and TCC.  No file
+from this 21-file pass remains deferred.  Five implementations remain: the
+already-recorded checksum hard case and the four special compilation cases.
+
+### Special compilation cases
+
+`prefix.c`, `version.c`, and `toplev.c` were not source incompatibilities.
+Their conventional Makefile rules supply configuration macros which the
+generic chunk probe did not:
+
+```text
+prefix.c   PREFIX, BASEVER
+version.c  BASEVER, DATESTAMP, DEVPHASE
+toplev.c   TARGET_NAME
+```
+
+The chunk generator now reads the same values from the configured Makefile and
+the three GCC version files, defines them immediately before the affected
+include, and undefines them immediately afterward.  This preserves the normal
+per-translation-unit scope and avoids hard-coding the configured installation
+prefix or target.  `prefix.c` joined direct chunk 049, `version.c` joined
+backend chunk 001, and `toplev.c` joined backend chunk 072.
+
+TCC initially rejected this declaration in `config/i386/i386.c`:
+
+```c
+const struct attribute_spec ix86_attribute_table[];
+```
+
+The declaration precedes the complete definition later in the same file and
+is intended only to make the symbol visible to target-hook macros.  GCC accepts
+it as a tentative incomplete-array definition, but TCC 0.9.27 diagnoses
+`unknown type size`.  Adding `extern` expresses the intended declaration
+unambiguously, is valid for both compilers, and does not change the later
+definition.  A conventional GCC rebuild of `i386.o` and `cc1` succeeds after
+the change.
+
+Individually, both `toplev.c` and the corrected `i386.c` fitted backend chunk
+072.  Combining them exposed deliberate repeated inclusions of `except.h` and
+`recog.h`.  The build was fixed forward by splitting the original pair:
+chunk 072 now contains `tree-ssa-operands.c` plus `toplev.c`, while new chunk
+083 contains `tree-ssa-alias.c` plus `i386.c`.  Both resulting chunks compile
+with GCC and TCC.
+
+The final attempted state is 286/287.  GCC- and TCC-produced hybrid executables
+and the rebuilt conventional compiler are native i386 binaries.  All three
+compile the smoke input to byte-identical assembly with SHA-256:
+
+```text
+c2be4b9929ebf7ed0ec4a4e437ea5181c9a251df09c5b73d6305aac24f45f548
+```
+
+The only outstanding hard case is the generated `cc1-checksum.c` entry already
+recorded in the hard-case ledger above.  Every other required implementation
+has been attempted and integrated.
+
+### Regression-driven final state
+
+The statement immediately above records the first complete-attempt build, not
+the accepted final state.  The required full regression run subsequently
+proved the `reload.c` placement semantically unsafe, as recorded immediately
+in the hard-case ledger.  After removing only `reload.c`, targeted compile and
+vector reproducers passed and a second complete GCC C testsuite run matched the
+checked baseline exactly:
+
+```text
+PASS          42139
+XFAIL           116
+FAIL              0
+XPASS             0
+UNRESOLVED         1
+UNTESTED          28
+UNSUPPORTED      293
+ERROR              0
+```
+
+The accepted final state is therefore 285/287 (99.30 percent), with two fully
+documented hard cases.  It builds with both GCC and TCC, while the conventional
+tree still rebuilds with the `i386.c` declaration correction.
+
+Three warm-cache matched-set `-O0 -g` GCC timings for all 285 migrated files
+were:
+
+```text
+mode            runs (seconds)        median
+conventional    58.23 60.66 59.61     59.61
+unified         38.63 39.93 39.84     39.84
+```
+
+The unified form saves 19.77 seconds, reduces wall time by 33.16 percent, and
+is 1.50 times as fast for the exact migrated set.  Archive rewriting, linking,
+and the two conventional hard cases are excluded from both sides.
