+# True single-translation-unit cc1 work log — 2026-08-01
+
+## 2026-08-01 21:48 BST — correction of the build model
+
+The prior port compiled 285 implementation files through 86 separate unity
+compiler invocations: 83 manifest-defined chunks, `cc1_unified.c`, and two
+libdecnumber drivers.  That batching was useful for finding compatible groups,
+but it did not implement the requested single translation unit.  The previous
+speed measurements consequently describe batched unity compilation and must
+not be presented as measurements of the final design.
+
+The corrected target is one checked-in `gcc/cc1_unified.c` containing the
+complete i686-linux cc1 implementation closure, compiled by one invocation of
+the selected C compiler.  The normal source build must remain usable.
+
+The build wrapper must also stop invoking `configure`.  Host and target facts,
+generated machine-description files, generated GC metadata, and component
+configuration required by this fixed i686-linux target will be checked into a
+fixed configuration directory.  Refresh tooling may consume an existing
+configured developer tree, but the user-facing unified build must not run
+Autoconf tests or depend on that tree.
+
+## First complete GCC-source probe
+
+All existing backend and direct chunks were presented to one compiler process
+using the conventional configured tree only as a diagnostic input.  The first
+errors were repeated definitions from historically unguarded `recog.h`,
+`except.h`, and `reload.h`.  Ordinary include guards were added; these preserve
+the conventional build and implement the desired include-once header behavior.
+
+The next probe exposed the same issue in `domwalk.h` and `cselib.h`, followed
+by genuine file-scope collisions such as `copy_bb`, `init_tmp_var`,
+`save_tmp_var`, `reg_dies`, and `reg_becomes_live`.  The intended resolution is
+to give colliding file-local identifiers per-implementation names at the
+amalgamation boundary while leaving each conventional translation unit
+unchanged.  Header guards and source-local renames are distinct: guards apply
+to recursive interface headers, while deliberate multi-include `.def` files
+must remain repeatable.
+
+## 2026-08-01 22:25 BST — complete translation unit and first runtime test
+
+The user clarified that build-generated files must not be committed, with the
+sole exception of fixed Autoconf compatibility headers.  Accordingly, no
+generated instruction, GC, option, manifest, object, or executable output will
+be added to Git.  The standalone build will generate machine-description and
+GC inputs beneath its out-of-tree artifact directory.
+
+The implementation closure now consists of 287 direct source includes in
+`gcc/cc1_unified.c`.  A single invocation of the Etch system GCC successfully
+compiled the entire file to one object.  This exposed and resolved the final
+GCC-core tag collision and support-library collisions in libcpp,
+libdecnumber, and libiberty.
+
+The first linked executable printed its GCC 4.2.1 version but crashed while
+opening its assembly output.  A debugger showed unbounded recursion in
+libiberty's `fopen_unlocked`: `gcc/system.h` maps `fopen` to the unlocked
+wrapper, and that translation-unit-local macro had leaked into the wrapper's
+own fallback implementation.  The amalgamation now drops that macro
+immediately before including `fopen_unlocked.c`.
+
+A second semantic leak was found from `tree-nested.c`, which intentionally
+poisons `create_tmp_var` until the end of its conventional translation unit.
+The repeatable unified boundary header now ends that poison at the equivalent
+source boundary.  After these fixes, the one-object cc1 links and compiles a
+trivial C program to a non-empty assembly file.
+
+This summary was recorded 37 minutes after the preceding 21:48 BST summary.
+The conventional out-of-tree `make -j1 all-gcc` build was then rerun and
+completed successfully, confirming that the source remains usable in its
+ordinary non-unified mode.
+
+## 2026-08-01 22:48 BST — fixed configuration and empty-tree builds
+
+This summary was recorded 23 minutes after the preceding 22:25 BST summary.
+
+The old wrapper was replaced.  It no longer runs top-level or component
+`configure`, invokes Make, creates unity chunks, or depends on a configured
+build tree.  It copies the checked-in Etch/i686 compatibility headers into its
+artifact directory, builds the ordinary GCC generator programs, and emits all
+instruction, option, and GC outputs beneath `unified-artifacts`.  These outputs
+are not tracked.  The maintained gengtype input inventory and its small AWK
+formatter replace the language lists which configure normally places in the
+GCC Makefile.
+
+A build from a genuinely empty out-of-tree directory completed using Etch GCC
+4.1.2, linked cc1, and passed the C-to-assembly smoke test.  Its one `-O0 -g`
+compile of `cc1_unified.c` took 27 seconds.  The same empty-directory build,
+using system GCC only for build-machine generators and TCC 0.9.27 for
+`cc1_unified.c` and the link, also passed; TCC's one invocation took 2 seconds.
+A final independent empty-directory GCC build with the completed wrapper also
+passed and measured 29 seconds for the one implementation compile.
+
+TCC exposed two ordering defects hidden by GCC's permissive implicit-function
+handling.  `global.c` called `cannot_change_mode_set_regs` before the later
+`regclass.c` definition, so the unified driver now supplies the real prototype
+at that boundary.  Separately, `ifcvt.c`'s local default definition of
+`HAVE_conditional_trap` prevented `optabs.c` from defining its matching
+`gen_conditional_trap` fallback.  The repeatable source boundary now drops both
+conditional-trap macros, reproducing conventional end-of-translation-unit
+behavior.
