+# Standalone unified cc1 build work log — 2026-08-01
+
+## Problem
+
+`build-cc1-unified.sh` originally assumed that its current directory was an
+already configured, conventionally built GCC tree.  From a new directory it
+therefore stopped immediately with:
+
+```text
+current directory is not a configured GCC build
+```
+
+That contradicted the intended unified workflow: users should not have to pay
+for a conventional `all-gcc` build before compiling the unified compiler.
+
+## Resolution
+
+The wrapper now configures an empty out-of-tree directory itself.  It invokes
+only the standard GCC configure and Make machinery needed to:
+
+1. configure GCC, libcpp, libdecnumber, and target libiberty;
+2. build the build-machine libiberty used by GCC's generator programs;
+3. generate the configuration, option, RTL, machine-description, and localedir
+   inputs included by the unified translation units;
+4. compile conventional `reload.o`, which remains an order-sensitive hard
+   case; and
+5. let the normal `cc1` link rule produce `cc1-checksum.o` and perform the
+   final link against minimal unified archives.
+
+The four target support archives are created directly under
+`unified-artifacts`; they are no longer copied from conventional builds.
+Consequently the standalone path does not run `all-gcc`, create a conventional
+`gcc/cc1`, or create full conventional target support archives.
+
+System `/usr/bin/gcc` is deliberately used for configuration, GCC's
+build-machine generators, the remaining conventional object, and the final
+link.  `CC` continues to select the compiler for the unified objects.  This
+separation makes both the stock Etch compiler and `/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc`
+valid unified front ends without asking TCC to build GCC's generator suite.
+
+## TCC-only link issue
+
+The first standalone TCC link had unresolved `C_alloca`, `libiberty_optr`,
+`libiberty_nptr`, and `libiberty_len` references.  TCC lowers the relevant
+stack allocation to the C fallback in `libiberty/alloca.c`; the system-GCC
+unified objects did not need it.  The wrapper now builds `alloca.o` explicitly
+and adds it to the minimal unified libiberty archive.  This is part of GCC
+4.2.1's existing source and standard build machinery, not an added dependency.
+
+## Validation
+
+A new GCC build directory completed in one wrapper invocation in 107.67
+seconds wall time and produced a native i386 `unified-artifacts/cc1_unified`.
+No conventional `gcc/cc1` or full conventional target support archive existed
+afterward.  The compiler successfully compiled the existing torture-test smoke
+input at `-O0`.
+
+The same one-command empty-directory validation was performed with TCC in a
+second new build directory.  It completed in 87.24 seconds wall time, produced
+a native i386 executable, and likewise left no conventional `gcc/cc1` or full
+target support archive.  Both standalone compilers compiled
+`gcc.c-torture/compile/20000804-1.c` at `-O0`; their assembly was byte-identical
+with SHA-256:
+
+```text
+762487823b3ae8906acd074153399329b2f511bcc0c86689079585f78ad8f105
+```
