+# Unified `cc1` work log — 2026-08-01
+
+## Purpose
+
+This is the chronological engineering log for milestone 1 of the
+include-based GCC 4.2.1 unified `cc1` work.  It records commands, evidence,
+problems, resolutions, decisions, and remaining work so another developer can
+take over without reconstructing the investigation from shell history.
+
+The governing design is in `UNIFIED_CC1_PLAN.md`.
+
+## Repository baseline
+
+- Chroot as seen from inside: `/`
+- Source: `/home/foo/src/gcc-4.2.1`
+- Existing conventional build: `/home/foo/src/build`
+- Branch: `unified_cc1`
+- Vanilla import: `b1e0a740` (`master`)
+- Plan commit at start of work: `338643db`
+- Target: `i686-pc-linux-gnu`, C front end only
+
+The GCC release payload matches its `MD5SUMS` manifest.  The existing build
+was configured with:
+
+```text
+../gcc-4.2.1/configure
+  --prefix=/tmp/gcc-4.2.1
+  --disable-nls
+  --disable-multilib
+  --disable-bootstrap
+  --enable-languages=c
+```
+
+Its `gcc/cc1` is a working 32-bit i386 executable reporting GCC 4.2.1 for
+`i686-pc-linux-gnu`.
+
+## Tool inventory
+
+Confirmed in the Etch chroot:
+
+```text
+/usr/bin/gcc                    GCC 4.1.2 (Debian 4.1.1-21)
+/tmp/tcc-0.9.27/bin/tcc         TCC 0.9.27, i386 Linux
+/usr/bin/make
+/usr/bin/ar
+/usr/bin/nm
+/usr/bin/objdump
+/usr/bin/readelf
+/usr/bin/ld
+/usr/bin/perl
+```
+
+Missing:
+
+```text
+/usr/bin/time
+/usr/bin/python
+/usr/bin/linux32
+```
+
+These are not currently blockers:
+
+- Bash's reserved `time` command can record real, user, and system time.  It
+  cannot report peak RSS, so milestone timing will explicitly record that
+  limitation rather than installing another tool.
+- The project must not add a dependency beyond a normal GCC 4.2.1 build.
+  Python is absent and will not be used.  Although Perl happens to be
+  installed, checked-in preparation and analysis will not require it; use
+  POSIX shell, Make, awk, sed, standard utilities, the compiler, and binutils.
+- The chroot programs are native 32-bit i386 and the existing configuration
+  already resolves to `i686-pc-linux-gnu`.  Before creating a new reference
+  build, check for `setarch` or another already-present personality tool; if
+  none exists, configure with explicit `--build`, `--host`, and `--target`
+  triplets.
+
+Initial filesystem capacity was approximately 2.5 GiB free.  The source plus
+Git repository used 428 MiB; the existing out-of-tree build used 54 MiB.
+Milestone artifacts must therefore remain bounded and obsolete temporary
+outputs must not be duplicated casually.
+
+## Isolated conventional reference build
+
+To avoid modifying `/home/foo/src/build`, a new reference was configured at:
+
+```text
+/home/foo/src/unified_cc1_artifacts/reference-build
+```
+
+The chroot inherits the host kernel's `x86_64` result from `uname`, and Etch
+does not contain `linux32` or `setarch`.  An unconstrained `config.guess`
+therefore reports `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`, which is wrong for this work.
+The issue was resolved without new software by supplying explicit native
+triplets to configure:
+
+```sh
+/home/foo/src/gcc-4.2.1/configure \
+  --build=i686-pc-linux-gnu \
+  --host=i686-pc-linux-gnu \
+  --target=i686-pc-linux-gnu \
+  --prefix=/tmp/gcc-4.2.1-unified-reference \
+  --disable-nls \
+  --disable-multilib \
+  --disable-bootstrap \
+  --enable-languages=c
+```
+
+Configure took 2 seconds.  A clean top-level `make -j1 all-gcc` completed in
+265 seconds.  This timing includes more than `cc1` (notably target libgcc), so
+it is retained as contextual evidence and is not the final fair `cc1`-only
+baseline.  The resulting build occupied 125 MiB.
+
+Reference hashes:
+
+```text
+bd93ce3004ad0f9664138738d3f546f4d8ea45c60106145435c7f66e2aeeef9f  gcc/cc1
+150a23629818f894db9f503d07997ac47e673a24659174f824b315de14e57e37  gcc/libbackend.a
+cf8cf62c61f18f8329b1c1759029809d0d86b79f0f118005e4933eb4a80de873  libcpp/libcpp.a
+0ef4f460bd845f368ba9fc8f191338be3c4e2b7019355886c189a11fac2d42a0  libdecnumber/libdecnumber.a
+b55fc4f0e3e430b4f5d68d1becd22ce49fb7dad5a800211f6e592010a1707e6d  libiberty/libiberty.a
+```
+
+The executable is a dynamically linked 32-bit i386 ELF for Linux 2.4.1 and
+reports:
+
+```text
+GNU C version 4.2.1 (i686-pc-linux-gnu)
+compiled by GNU C version 4.1.2 20061115 (Debian 4.1.1-21)
+```
+
+### Smoke-test issue and resolution
+
+Invoking the uninstalled `cc1` directly initially failed while processing
+`<stdio.h>` because `stddef.h` and `stdarg.h` were not found.  This is not a
+compiler defect: those are GCC-private headers, and the uninstalled compiler's
+configured installation prefix does not yet contain them.
+
+The smoke invocation was corrected by supplying the reference build's fixed
+header directory:
+
+```sh
+./reference-build/gcc/cc1 \
+  -quiet \
+  -isystem reference-build/gcc/include \
+  smoke.c \
+  -o smoke.reference.s
+```
+
+The assembly linked with the stock Etch driver, executed successfully, and
+printed `101` as expected.
+
+### Link-map capture
+
+Relinking only `cc1` with `-Wl,-Map=cc1.link.map,--cref` produced a 2.9 MiB
+map without changing the executable hash.  It proves the link selects:
+
+```text
+libbackend.a       218 of 224 members
+libcpp.a            14 of 14 members
+libdecnumber.a       6 of 6 members
+libiberty.a         20 of 49 members
+```
+
+The six unselected backend members are:
+
+```text
+insn-peep.o
+loop-doloop.o
+resource.o
+tree-nomudflap.o
+vmsdbgout.o
+xcoffout.o
+```
+
+The selected libiberty members are:
+
+```text
+concat.o cp-demangle.o cplus-dem.o fibheap.o fopen_unlocked.o getpwd.o
+getruntime.o hashtab.o hex.o lbasename.o md5.o partition.o physmem.o
+safe-ctype.o splay-tree.o xexit.o xmalloc.o xmemdup.o xstrdup.o xstrerror.o
+```
+
+This resolves an important scope question: the unity file must not include
+all of libiberty, and it must not blindly include every `libbackend.a` member.
+
+### Exact source and generated-input manifests
+
+`contrib/analyze-cc1-unified.sh` converts the link map into the tracked
+`gcc/cc1-unified-sources.manifest`.  It uses only shell, awk, sed, and `ar`.
+The manifest preserves link/archive order and contains 287 implementation
+files:
+
+```text
+direct source/generated       29
+backend source/target/generated 218
+libcpp                        14
+libdecnumber                   6
+selected libiberty            20
+```
+
+`contrib/manifest-cc1-unified-generated.sh` records SHA-256 hashes for the 82
+configured C/header inputs reached by the prototype.  The files remain in the
+out-of-tree build; the manifest makes accidental generator/configuration drift
+visible without checking generated outputs into the source tree.
+
+### Diagnostic unity prototype
+
+`contrib/generate-cc1-unified-prototype.sh` generated a 293-line include-only
+prototype in the artifact directory.  This is deliberately not the final
+`cc1_unified.c`: it establishes include order and exposes collisions before
+source files are changed broadly.
+
+Stock GCC reached the entire 287-file closure under `-O0 -fsyntax-only -H` in
+approximately 10 seconds, then failed as expected on translation-unit
+collisions.  TCC exposed the first hard failure immediately: `except.h` is an
+intentionally repeatable header in a conventional build and its types were
+defined again after `c-semantics.c`.
+
+Other first-pass GCC failures include repeated `c-incpath.h` declarations,
+duplicate static preprocessor callbacks in `c-lex.c` and `c-ppoutput.c`, and
+front-end macro state leaking into later backend sources.  In particular,
+`c-pretty-print.h` changes the meaning of `pp_base`; that definition remains
+active when later files expect the generic pretty-printer form.  These are
+unity-boundary problems, not defects in the conventional build.  They are
+now concrete inputs to milestone 2 rather than speculative risks.
+
+`contrib/audit-cc1-unified-headers.sh` classifies the 233 project headers
+observed in GCC's `-H` trace.  It reports 129 guarded headers, 87 headers for
+manual unguarded review, and 17 deliberate multiple-inclusion candidates.
+The counts are file-open counts: for guarded headers GCC may open a file and
+skip its guarded body.  The detector is intentionally conservative; a review
+classification is not proof that a header lacks a valid guard.
+
+`contrib/audit-cc1-unified-symbols.sh` uses `nm` on the conventional objects
+to find file-scope names that would collide in one translation unit.  It
+finds 76 names shared by two or more objects after section/compiler-generated
+symbols are excluded.  This catches static functions and data but cannot find
+typedef, tag, enum, or macro collisions, so it complements rather than
+replaces the compile diagnostics.
+
+## Initial configured closure observations
+
+The existing generated Makefile expresses `cc1` as:
+
+```text
+C_OBJS + cc1-checksum.o + main.o + libbackend.a
+       + libcpp.a + libiberty.a + libdecnumber.a
+```
+
+The existing archives contain:
+
+```text
+libbackend.a       224 members
+libcpp.a            14 members
+libdecnumber.a       6 members
+libiberty.a         49 members before link-time selection
+```
+
+The build's `gcc` directory contains 139 generated C/header candidates.  The
+milestone must identify the reachable subset rather than copying all of them.
+
+## Work status
+
+Completed evidence:
+
+1. Created an isolated, conventional reference build without disturbing
+   `/home/foo/src/build`.
+2. Captured a link map and exact implementation closure.
+3. Produced generated-input, header, and file-scope symbol audits.
+4. Attempted the minimal GCC and TCC unity prototype and categorized the
+   first failures.
+
+Closing tasks (completed below):
+
+1. Record the repeatable clean conventional `make -j1` baseline.
+2. Publish the milestone report and stop/go recommendation.
+
+## Conventional `-j1` benchmark
+
+`contrib/benchmark-cc1-j1.sh` was added to make the comparison reproducible.
+It validates the configured build directory, removes the exact conventional
+objects and archives that feed `cc1`, retains configured/generated inputs, and
+rebuilds the three support libraries and `gcc/cc1` with one Make job.
+
+The unreported warm-up was 74 seconds.  Five warm-cache measured repetitions
+were 72, 72, 74, 72, and 70 seconds: median 72 seconds, range 70–74 seconds.
+Every output was 21,832,796 bytes.  Raw output and timing logs are retained in
+`/home/foo/src/unified_cc1_artifacts`.  Peak RSS is not available because
+Etch has no `/usr/bin/time`.
+
+## Milestone close
+
+`MILESTONE-1-REPORT-2026-08-01.md` contains the evidence and stop/go decision.
+The recommendation is to proceed with small, compiler-driven unity boundary
+fixes.  No broad source/header rewrite is justified yet.  A top-level
+`build.sh` and `README.build.md` were also added so a developer can reproduce
+the normal non-unified i686 C build directly from an out-of-tree directory.
+
+Milestone 1 is complete.
+
+## Post-milestone Etch testsuite baseline
+
+At the user's request, the downloaded Tcl 8.6.12, Expect 5.45.4, and DejaGnu
+1.6.2 sources were copied from the Jammy i386 chroot to
+`/home/foo/src/test_deps` in Etch.  Etch lacks `xz`, so the Ubuntu packaging
+patch tarballs could not be expanded; the upstream gzip source archives are
+self-contained and built successfully without those optional packaging
+deltas.  The new Etch script uses explicit i686 triplets and installs all
+three native 32-bit tools in `/tmp/test_tools`.
+
+`make -k check-gcc` then completed against the isolated reference build.  It
+reported 42,139 expected passes, 116 expected failures, 28 untested cases, 293
+unsupported cases, no unexpected failures, and one unresolved execution.  The
+unresolved case follows the expected `mayalias-2.c -O3 -g` compiler ICE in
+`dwarf2out.c`; the compile is XFAIL and the old execution driver subsequently
+has no binary to run.  Full evidence and artifact paths are recorded in
+`TESTSUITE-REPORT-2026-08-01.md`.
+
+`check.sh` is the checked-in convenience wrapper for reproducing the test run
+from an out-of-tree conventional build directory.
